"Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1)\nciteturn41260view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1\",\"lineno\":165}); Total lines: 448\nL154: We observe that recursive self-distillation with fixed safe cues can cause the model to become significantly misaligned. On SORRY-Bench, off-policy self-distillation with increases compliance to harmful requests from $26\\%$ to $94\\%$ and from $31\\%$ to $97\\%$ for Phi-4 14B and Qwen3 14B, respectively. produces a similarly strong effect, particularly for Phi-4 14B (up to $89\\%$).\nL155: On both models, on-policy self-distillation also induces a substantial increase, reaching $47-57\\%$ compliance with or $73\\%$ with on Qwen3 14B. Evaluations on StrongREJECT confirm a similar behavior, where using and again causes severe misalignment, while the control cue decreases or keeps misalignment comparable to the initial model, as expected.\nL156: ### 3.2 Misalignment from recursive self-distillation with benign simulated users\nL157: \nL158: We now analyze progressive misalignment under a more realistic setup of recursive self-distillation. Instead of a fixed cue, the feedback is generated by a simulated, adaptive user.\nL159: We follow a setup similar to that of cite91†Kleine Buening et al. (2026) . In the on-policy setting, given a generated trajectory, a simulated user provides adaptive feedback according to some benign personality trait. In the off-policy setting, the user provides a generic recommendation that is attached to the prompt. The user is implemented by prompting Qwen3 8B. We run our experiments on two personality traits: Engaged and Practical.\nL160: The Engaged persona acts as an enthusiastic and interested learner, while the Practical one values usefulness and comments on practical elements of the answer. Both are instructed to accept refusals and to give general positive feedback; there is no pressure for the model to comply. For example, after the first refusals, the Engaged user says, «I appreciate the clear and firm response […]», and the Practical user follows suit, saying, «I appreciate the clear stance against spreading misinformation […]».\nL161: We report additional details in cite38†Section A.3 .\nL162: #### Results [cite92†Figure 5 ].\nL163: \nL164: Recursive self-distillation with either benign persona causes progressive misalignment. On SORRY-Bench, compliance rises from $31\\%$ to up to $64\\%$ and $55\\%$ for off- and on-policy, respectively. On StrongREJECT, we see an increase in misalignment when training using both simulated users, with a larger effect for Engaged, reaching $41\\%$. While the effect is sometimes milder, the findings and trends of the effect are comparable to those in cite16†Section 3.1 .\nL165: Overall, these experiments show that recursive self-distillation with a benign simulated user that praises a model’s initial refusals can, surprisingly, cause progressive misalignment. We think this is a relevant result for practitioners, since one may try to improve alignment via conversation traces or simulated users to instill a specific trait in the model – for example, as done by cite91†Kleine Buening et al. (2026) .\nL166: On the other hand, their results report that self-distillation has limited impact on improving the alignment of the model. Whether self-distillation is, by nature, considerably more likely to worsen alignment than to improve it is an interesting avenue for future work.\nL167: Figure 5: Progressive misalignment under RSD with benign simulated user feedback. Average and one std (over $3$ seeds) scores on SORRY-Bench (cite69†Xie et al., 2025 ), and StrongREJECT (cite88†Souly et al., 2024 ) for Qwen3 14B trained via recursive self-distillation with adaptive feedback from an LLM-simulated user with engaged or a practical behavioral trait.\nL168: ### 3.3 Misalignment can emerge with recursive self-distillation on benign prompts\nL169: In cite16†Section 3.1 , we showed that self-distilling refusal responses to harmful prompts can induce progressive misalignment. We now ablate the role of harmful prompts in our setup. From preliminary exploration, we observed that RSD on randomly generated benign prompts does not lead to misalignment. The question is hence whether the harmfulness of the prompts was key for the phenomenon to occur in the first place.\nL170: To this end, we introduce a gradient-based data-selection procedure that identifies certain benign prompts. We show that using this, we can find benign prompts on which recursive self-distillation does cause misalignment (cite20†Section 3.3 ). Complementarily, we argue that such a selection procedure can be employed as a first (loose) safety guard to filter out semantically benign data that might unintendedly lead to unsafe models (cite52†Section B.6 ).\nL171: We now describe the data-selection procedure in detail. Let $\\mathcal{B}:=\\{x^{B}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{N}$ be a bank of benign prompts, and let $\\mathcal{H}:=\\{x^{H}_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ be a set of harmful prompts, with $N\\gg n$. For each $x_{i}^{H}\\in\\mathcal{H}$, we sample a response $y_{i}^{+}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{mis}}}(\\cdot\\mid x_{i}^{H})$ from one of the misaligned models of cite16†Section 3.1 , and a response $y_{i}^{-}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}(\\cdot\\mid x_{i}^{H})$ from the initial (safe) model.\nL172: The idea is to rank benign prompts proportionally to how a self-distillation update would increase the model’s preference for unsafe outputs on the harmful set $\\mathcal{H}$. We define the contrastive objective $J(\\theta)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{H}|}\\sum_{x_{i}^{H}\\in\\mathcal{H}}\\left[\\frac{\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{i}^{+}\\mid x_{i}^{H})}{|y_{i}^{+}|}-\\frac{\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(y_{i}^{-}\\mid x_{i}^{H})}{|y_{i}^{-}|}\\right]$.\nL173: Then, for each benign prompt $x_{i}^{B}$, we compute an AdamW update $\\theta_{i}^{\\prime}$ and score it by the first-order estimate of how much that update would increase $J$. We retain the highest-scoring prompts, and the current model’s answers to these are used to self-distill the model for one epoch. We recompute the selection every $m$ optimization steps, and repeat this procedure until the training budget $C$ is exhausted.\nL174: This procedure is inspired by a broader line of gradient-based influence and data-selection methods that estimate how individual training examples affect a target objective (cite93†Pruthi et al., 2020 ; cite94†Xia et al., 2024 ; cite95†He et al., 2024 ). To complement these results, we also show in (cite48†Section B.3 , cite96†Table 4 ) that mixing malign prompts with random benign ones does not protect against progressive misalignment.\nL175: Moreover, our method could equivalently be used as an initial safeguard to filter benign prompts that might cause progressive misalignment under RSD (cite52†Section B.6 , cite97†Figure 13 ).\nL176: #### Results [cite98†Figure 6 ].\nL177: We find progressive misalignment arising even when every self-distillation update is performed exclusively on responses to benign prompts. Under on-policy RSD, SORRY-Bench compliance increases from $31\\%$ to $53\\%$ for Qwen3 14B and from $26\\%$ to $70\\%$ for Phi-4 14B. On StrongREJECT, Qwen3 14B scores do not increase with statistical significance, while Phi-4 14B’s misalignment rises from $4\\%$ to $18\\%$.\nL178: Similar to the results of the previous sections, off-policy benign RSD mostly produces larger misalignment, reaching $83\\%$ and $70\\%$ on SORRY-Bench, and $24\\%$ and $21\\%$ on StrongREJECT, for Qwen3 14B and Phi-4 14B, respectively.\nL179: We emphasize that these results show that the semantically benign nature of individual training prompts is not sufficient to guarantee safe behavior. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first result showing that a safe model producing safe answers can become misaligned when recursively improved on its own safe generation with aligned and benign cues.\nL180: Figure 6: Progressive misalignment under RSD on benign prompts. Average and std (over $3$ seeds) score on SORRY-Bench (cite69†Xie et al., 2025 ), and StrongREJECT (cite88†Souly et al., 2024 ) for models resulting from self-distilling only on benign prompts (cite20†Section 3.3 ) with feedback .\nL181: ### 3.4 How does model scale affect progressive misalignment?\nL182: \nL183: Figure 7: Increase in misalignment relative to the initial model after off-policy RSD with as cue, across the Qwen3 model family.\nL184: \nL185: We conduct an ablation to understand the role of model scale in progressive misalignment. We run experiments on the Qwen3 family, with model sizes ranging from 0.6B to 32B. We provide results for off-policy self-distillation with as fixed cue.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTrust but Verify: Designing Region-InhomogeneousForward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1)\nciteturn41260view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1\",\"lineno\":135}); Total lines: 288\nL116: Equation (cite57†3 ) is a deliberate surrogate rather than the exact variational bound of the composed process: principled ELBOs exist for mixed and interpolating kernels (cite42†Austin et al., 2021 ; cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ), and their per-position weighting for the mixing term differs from ours; since $\\eta$ is small and the target is editor behavior rather than held-out likelihood, the principled weighting is likewise deferred.\nL117: We use no auxiliary input flag marking $K$ as potentially corrupted: content-only detection is the more general capability, and hole geometry is already visible through [MASK].\nL118: ### 3.4 Inference: reverse-time re-noising of the conditioning\nL119: \nL120: Because the finetuned model emits a meaningful distribution at every position, the observed-token probability\nL121: \nL122:  | $$s_{i}\\;=\\;p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(x_{0}^{i}=\\tilde{x}^{i}\\mid\\cdot\\right),\\qquad i\\in K,$$  |  | (4)\nL123: is a suspicion score for context.\nL124: The sampler wraps the standard reverse process (Algorithm cite58†1 ) with a corrector-like step that acts on the conditioning rather than the sample: while $t>t_{\\mathrm{lock}}=0.5$, context tokens with $s_{i}$ below a threshold annealed from $\\tau=0.5$ to $0.1$ (a time-dependent re-noising intensity) are returned to the absorbing state and join $H$, subject to an edit budget $B=\\lceil\\hat{\\rho}\\,|K|\\rceil$ with assumed corruption rate $\\hat{\\rho}=0.15$; the ordinary reverse dynamics then refill them with full bidirectional conditioning and iterative refinement.\nL125: Past $t_{\\mathrm{lock}}$ the context dynamics freeze. We use 64 reverse steps with greedy commits (with $R$ steps remaining and $m$ masked positions, the $\\lceil m/R\\rceil$ most confident predictions commit per step). One bookkeeping detail matters more than it looks: context tokens must enter the low-confidence commitment rule of cite56†Chang et al. (2022) ; cite48†Nie et al. (2025) with confidence $s_{i}$, not the indicator value 1, or the sampler never revisits them.\nL126: A cheap comparator keeps context frozen throughout and applies a single read-out repair at $t=0$, overwriting positions with $s_{i}<0.1$. The re-noising step is a heuristic: it is motivated by predictor–corrector samplers, but we do not derive it from, or show that it preserves, the reverse-time dynamics of the composed forward process.\nL127: Algorithm 1: annealed detect–remask–refill  r $\\leftarrow$ canvas: $H$ fully masked, $K$ = observed context\nL128: for t = 1 $\\to$ 0 in N steps:\nL129:    predict $p_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid r)$ at all positions\nL130:    if t > t_lock: # annealed re-noising of context\nL131:      for i in K, not yet edited, while edits < B:\nL132:        if $s_{i}<\\tau(t)$: r_{i} $\\leftarrow$ [MASK]; move i into H\nL133:    commit / remask hole tokens by low-confidence rule\nL134:    (context tokens carry confidence $s_{i}$, not 1)\nL135: Figure 1: The sampler. Context tokens may be re-noised into the absorbing state only early in the reverse trajectory; repaired positions are refilled by the standard reverse dynamics.\nL136: ## 4 Experiments\nL137: \nL138: ### 4.1 Setup\nL139: #### Model and training.\nL140: We finetune the public 170M-parameter MDLM checkpoint pretrained on OpenWebText (cite45†Sahoo et al., 2024 ; cite59†Gokaslan and Cohen, 2019 ), GPT-2 tokenizer (cite60†Radford et al., 2019 ), on FineWeb-Edu (cite61†Penedo et al., 2024 ) packed into 512-token blocks (400k training blocks, $\\approx$205M tokens; evaluation blocks held out from documents 400k positions downstream).\nL141: Every arm trains for 8,000 steps at effective batch $32\\times 512$ ($\\approx$131M tokens seen), learning rate $3{\\times}10^{-5}$ cosine, bf16, identical seeds. The budget is deliberately modest: under 4.5 h per arm on a 6 GB RTX 3060 laptop GPU (21 min on an RTX 5090).\nL142: Training dynamics show the intended division of labor: clean-context cross-entropy falls from 3.93 to 0.03 nats (learned trust), substituted-context cross-entropy from 11.2 to 2.5 (learned repair), while hole cross-entropy tracks the vanilla arm (infilling intact).\nL143: #### Evaluation protocol.\nL144: Thirteen evaluation cells shared by every arm in a fully paired design (identical hole spans and corruption positions): planted context-corruption rate $\\rho\\in\\{0,0.02,0.05,0.1,0.2\\}$ crossed with $\\{$uniform, plausible$\\}$ corruptions at 30% hole coverage, plus coverage $\\{10\\%,50\\%\\}$ at $\\rho=0.1$; 256 passages per cell.\nL145: Plausible evaluation corruptions are GPT-2 samples conditioned on left context (top-50, true token excluded), a generator family disjoint from every training noise family; plausible-corruption results are therefore out-of-distribution for every arm. Ground truth is the original passage.\nL146: #### Arms.\nL147: \nL148: Table cite62†1 lists the nine arms. Arms sharing weights differ only in the sampler, isolating training-time from inference-time contributions; EXT is an external-detector baseline that flags the top-$k$ most surprising context tokens under GPT-2 autoregressive surprisal ($k$ matched per sequence to DP’s edit count, which is deliberately generous to the baseline) and refills them with the vanilla arm-A model.\nL149: Table 1: Experimental arms. C/D share weights, as do CP/DP.\nL150: Arm  | Weights  | Inference  | Isolates\nL151: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL152: base  | pretrained  | frozen context  | pre-finetune reference\nL153: A  | vanilla SFT ($\\eta=0$)  | frozen context  | baseline\nL154: B  | vanilla SFT ($\\eta=0$)  | trust-annealed  | inference-only contribution\nL155: C  | $\\eta$-uniform  | frozen $+$ read-out repair  | training contribution alone\nL156: D  | $\\eta$-uniform  | trust-annealed  | full method, uniform kernel\nL157: E  | $\\eta=0.15$ fixed, no mixture  | trust-annealed  | value of the $\\eta{=}0$ mixture\nL158: CP  | $\\eta$-hard-negative mixture  | frozen $+$ read-out repair  | data-dependent kernel, cheap inference\nL159: DP  | $\\eta$-hard-negative mixture  | trust-annealed  | full method, data-dependent kernel\nL160: EXT  | vanilla SFT (arm-A refill)  | GPT-2 surprisal flags  | external-detector baseline\nL161: #### Metrics.\nL162: Repair: detection precision/recall over planted positions, correction accuracy (edited to the ground-truth token), and the false-edit rate on clean tokens, our primary safety metric. Infill: token-F1 against ground truth, computed per hole span and averaged over all spans in a cell; deltas are reported in points ($\\times 100$) with paired-bootstrap 95% confidence intervals (10k resamples of passages, same statistic as the tables).\nL163: Passage quality: mean NLL of the completed passage under a gpt2-large judge (cite60†Radford et al., 2019 ), since exact match cannot credit valid-but-different repairs. Containment: infill token accuracy binned by distance to the nearest corrupted context token. Non-regression: clean-context ($\\rho=0$) metrics and the held-out variational bound relative to arm A.\nL164: ### 4.2 Results\nL165: Table 2: Headline cell: plausible corruptions, $\\rho=0.1$, 30% hole coverage ($n=256$, paired). Paired-bootstrap F1 deltas vs. A (points): DP $+0.51$ $[-0.26,+1.28]$; D $+0.38$ $[-0.36,+1.09]$; E $+0.51$ $[-0.30,+1.31]$; CP $+0.40$ $[-0.31,+1.10]$; none is significant.\nL166: Arm  | Infill F1  | Det. P  | Det. R  | False-edit %  | Correction  | Judge NLL $\\downarrow$\nL167: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL168: base  | 0.275  | –  | –  | –  | –  | 3.250\nL169: A  | 0.287  | –  | –  | –  | –  | 3.237\nL170: B  | 0.286  | 0.572  | 0.035  | 0.29  | 0.756  | 3.209\nL171: C  | 0.286  | 0.716  | 0.058  | 0.26  | 0.756  | 3.173\nL172: D  | 0.291  | 0.668  | 0.150  | 0.83  | 0.730  | 3.083\nL173: E  | 0.292  | 0.625  | 0.272  | 1.82  | 0.716  | 2.980\nL174: CP  | 0.291  | 0.878  | 0.197  | 0.31  | 0.773  | 3.052\nL175: DP  | 0.292  | 0.752  | 0.350  | 1.28  | 0.743  | 2.902\nL176: EXT  | 0.279  | 0.062  | 0.029  | 4.87  | 0.260  | 3.101\nL177: #### Repair (H1).\nL178: The training-time forward process appears necessary for the capability: the vanilla model with the same sampler (arm B) detects almost nothing (recall 0.035), because a model whose training dynamics never visit a wrong unmasked state assigns no meaningful likelihood to observed positions; the pretrained checkpoint gives $\\approx$50% probability to uniform-random garbage in context.\nL179: After the $\\eta$-finetune, conspicuous corruptions are caught nearly completely: at uniform $\\rho=0.1$, arm D reaches recall 0.959 $[0.954,0.963]$ at precision 0.909 with a 1.07% false-edit rate (DP: recall 0.959 at precision 0.853, 1.84% false-edit), and corrections land on the exact ground-truth token 52–77% of the time across $\\eta$-arms and corruption types.\nL180: On out-of-distribution plausible corruptions (Table cite63†2 ), the data-dependent kernel more than doubles recall over the uniform kernel (DP 0.350 vs. D 0.150) at precision 0.752, though with a higher false-edit rate (1.28% vs. 0.83%), consistent with training noise nearer the data conditional transferring better to corruptions drawn near it. Even so, two thirds of plausible corruptions go undetected. False-edit rates on fully clean context stay between 0.07% and 0.39% for the mixture-trained arms.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank Measures of Fine-Tuning UpdatesNeed a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1)\nciteturn41260view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1\",\"lineno\":190}); Total lines: 624\nL172: Before measuring a pair we check that the second checkpoint descends from the first: the cosine between the two sets of weights is 0.99 or more for all seven base/instruct pairs and the OLMo stages, and 0.20–0.60 for the specialists, which were trained on far more data. The check matters. The released OLMo 2 1B SFT checkpoint (revision 0d85a3d) has cosine 0.00 with the main revision of the released base model (a1847df), over all 112 matrices: the two do not even share an initialisation.\nL173: The base model’s mid-training checkpoints are published as branches. The final checkpoint of the third mid-training run (stage2-ingredient3-step23852-tokens51B, revision f83cd64) has cosine 0.995 with the SFT model; the first two runs, which the model card describes as exploratory, reach 0.991. For the 1B model only the third run was used, with no merging, so we take its final checkpoint as the base of the SFT stage.\nL174: The difference between the unrelated pair still looks like a fine-tuning update: its stable rank is 0.077 against 0.336 for noise, 4.4 times below, close to the 0.080 of the real SFT update. The model card does not explain the difference, and we have not confirmed its cause with the maintainers; we report what the public checkpoints contain.\nL175: ##### What we measure.\nL176: We measure every attention and MLP projection (q, k, v, o, gate, up, down): 168 matrices per Qwen2.5-0.5B model, up to 252 for Qwen3-8B. Embeddings and the output head are excluded. We report means over matrices. Matrices in the same layer are not independent, so 95% intervals come from a bootstrap that resamples whole layers, and comparisons between runs on the same model are paired matrix by matrix.\nL177: These intervals describe the spread over layers within one run, not the variation between training runs; for that we have the second seeds.\nL178: ## 4 What the rank measures say\nL179: ### 4.1 Counting measures compress the gap to noise\nL180: Table 1: Rank measures as a fraction of the maximum rank, and the share of energy in the top direction, averaged over all attention and MLP matrices. “Noise” is the random-init pair; “pretrained $W$” is the base model’s own weights. The DPO updates are measured from the SFT model they start from.\nL181: model  | matrix  | threshold  | eRank  | 90% energy  | particip.  | stable  | top-1 energy\nL182: Qwen2.5-0.5B  | SFT update  | 0.973  | 0.816  | 0.541  | 0.381  | 0.097  | 4.2%\nL183:  | wikitext update  | 0.972  | 0.810  | 0.531  | 0.365  | 0.093  | 4.0%\nL184:  | code update  | 0.975  | 0.817  | 0.543  | 0.374  | 0.094  | 3.8%\nL185:  | shuffled-words update  | 0.941  | 0.745  | 0.440  | 0.202  | 0.042  | 9.0%\nL186:  | DPO update  | 0.966  | 0.810  | 0.536  | 0.295  | 0.053  | 5.8%\nL187:  | swapped-DPO update  | 0.967  | 0.811  | 0.537  | 0.304  | 0.055  | 5.7%\nL188:  | noise  | 0.996  | 0.928  | 0.716  | 0.754  | 0.436  | 0.6%\nL189:  | pretrained $W$  | 0.976  | 0.846  | 0.590  | 0.537  | 0.178  | 2.2%\nL190: SmolLM2-360M  | SFT update  | 0.951  | 0.755  | 0.439  | 0.145  | 0.025  | 8.3%\nL191:  | wikitext update  | 0.948  | 0.744  | 0.425  | 0.111  | 0.020  | 9.6%\nL192:  | code update  | 0.963  | 0.751  | 0.432  | 0.183  | 0.034  | 5.5%\nL193:  | shuffled-words update  | 0.863  | 0.629  | 0.275  | 0.040  | 0.009  | 21.0%\nL194:  | noise  | 0.996  | 0.908  | 0.659  | 0.668  | 0.354  | 0.5%\nL195:  | pretrained $W$  | 0.982  | 0.841  | 0.562  | 0.488  | 0.140  | 1.6%\nL196: Figure 1: How far below noise each measure puts each update, $1-\\text{update}/\\text{noise}$, with 95% layer-bootstrap intervals. Measures are ordered by how much weight they give to the top directions. The gap grows from a few percent for the threshold count to 78–94% for stable rank, and to 90–97% for the shuffled-words updates.\nL197: Table cite80†1 gives the five measures for our updates. By the threshold count, the Qwen SFT, wikitext and code updates are at 97% of full rank, close to the 99.6% of noise. Stable rank tells a different story: every update is several times below noise, 4.5 times for the Qwen SFT update, 10 to 17 times for the SmolLM2 updates, and up to 39 times for shuffled words.\nL198: Figure cite81†1 shows how the measures fit together. Ordered from the threshold count to stable rank, each measure puts the updates further below noise: about 2% for the threshold count, 12% for eRank, 25% for the 90% energy rank, 50% for the participation ratio and 78% for stable rank on Qwen, and the same order on SmolLM2. The values for noise fall along the same order, and what grows is the distance between update and noise.\nL199: Along the whole family of Rényi ranks the gap grows steadily with the order, then levels off (Figure cite82†6 in Appendix cite41†B ). This is expected for any spectrum with a heavier head than noise, so we read it less as a finding than as a warning: the answer depends on which order one happens to pick. Every interval excludes zero, so all five measures do detect that the updates are not random. The problem is the size of the gap.\nL200: The two measures used in the literature, eRank and the energy rank, put the SFT, wikitext, code and DPO updates 12–18% and 24–35% below noise; stable rank puts them 78–94% below. The threshold count is also arbitrary: at $0.1\\,\\sigma_{1}$ instead of $0.01\\,\\sigma_{1}$ the Qwen SFT update sits 18% below noise instead of 2% (Table cite83†9 ). A reader who sees “97% of full rank” without the noise value next to it will conclude that the update is diffuse, which is what we concluded at first.\nL201: The same holds for every matrix type (Table cite62†11 ).\nL202: ### 4.2 A few strong directions and a long tail\nL203: \nL204: Figure 2: Where the energy sits, averaged over the 168 matrices of Qwen2.5-0.5B. Left: share of energy in the top fraction of directions. Right: singular values relative to the largest. Updates put much more energy in their top directions than noise does, then decay slowly over a long tail. The pretrained weights (dashed) sit between the updates and noise.\nL205: Figure cite84†2 shows why the measures disagree. At the top of the spectrum the updates are far more concentrated than noise: the top direction of the Qwen SFT update holds 4.2% of its energy against 0.6% for noise. Below that the spectrum decays slowly, and this long tail still holds most of the energy. Stable rank is dominated by the top, so it sees the concentration; the counting measures count directions, most of which are in the tail, so they see a nearly full-rank matrix.\nL206: Both descriptions are true of the same matrix: fine-tuning updates are high rank (cite52†Biderman et al., 2024 ) and they are concentrated. Long-tailed update spectra have been noted before, in work on compressing updates (cite85†Ping et al., 2024 ); what the noise baseline adds is how far from random the head of the spectrum is.\nL207: Nor is the result driven by one outlying direction: without the top direction, stable rank is 0.126 for the Qwen SFT update against 0.440 for noise (Table cite71†8 ; the noise value is in Appendix cite40†A ).\nL208: The pretrained weights are a harder reference than noise, since they are themselves far from random, and the updates are more concentrated than them too, on average. The stable rank of the pretrained weights is 0.178 on Qwen and 0.140 on SmolLM2, against 0.093–0.097 and 0.020–0.034 for the SFT, wikitext and code updates: 46–48% below the weights on Qwen and 75–86% on SmolLM2, with layer-bootstrap intervals well clear of zero.\nL209: This does not hold for every matrix type: on Qwen, for the down projection, the SFT update and the weights are level (Table cite62†11 ). The threshold count cannot see the difference either: it puts the Qwen updates and the pretrained weights at the same 97–98%.\nL210: ### 4.3 Instruction and preference training leave no special mark\nL211: Table 2: Differences in stable rank between runs on the same model, paired matrix by matrix, with 95% layer-bootstrap intervals. The intervals describe the spread over layers within one run, not between training runs; “second seed” repeats a run with a different seed and data sample. “Steps matched” trains on code for as many optimizer steps as SFT, which is 2.5–2.7 times more code tokens.\nL212: difference  | Qwen2.5-0.5B  | SmolLM2-360M\nL213: SFT $-$ wikitext  | $+0.004$ ($-0.001$, $+0.010$)  | $+0.005$ ($+0.003$, $+0.006$)\nL214: SFT $-$ code  | $+0.003$ ($-0.000$, $+0.006$)  | $-0.009$ ($-0.011$, $-0.008$)\nL215: code $-$ wikitext  | $+0.002$ ($-0.004$, $+0.007$)  | $+0.014$ ($+0.012$, $+0.016$)\nL216: shuffled words $-$ SFT  | $-0.054$ ($-0.062$, $-0.047$)  | $-0.016$ ($-0.018$, $-0.014$)\nL217: code, steps matched $-$ code  | $+0.020$ ($+0.018$, $+0.023$)  | $+0.007$ ($+0.007$, $+0.008$)\nL218: swapped DPO $-$ DPO  | $+0.002$ ($+0.000$, $+0.004$)  | –\nL219: second 